  "transcript": "# Entrepreneurship and business\n\n## Cashflow is the immediate threat\n> Trades and small suppliers say running out of cash while waiting for payment is the biggest barrier to starting and growing.\n\nChair: Lee, what are the biggest barriers you see for people setting up and expanding trades-based businesses?\nLee Wilcox: Cashflow is number one. Trades often win work but struggle to fund materials before and wait to be paid after—chasing payments is relentless. Rising material prices are making that worse.\nLee Wilcox: That pressure feeds into stress and poor wellbeing. Construction already has among the highest suicide rates, and many tradespeople become business owners because they’re skilled workers, not trained managers.\nChair: Would tougher late‑payment rules help?\nLee Wilcox: They might. We also need to tackle 'rogue customers'—clients who delay or avoid payment. Trades rarely get paid quickly when work is done in people's homes.\n\n\n## How much it costs to start — from market stalls to shops\n> Markets give a low-cost route in for some, but setting up a street-food stall or buying/fitting a convenience shop needs significant capital and often personal guarantees.\n\nDan Aldridge: Are markets still an affordable route into business, and are they sustainable?\nJoe Harrison: Yes, markets remain an accessible entry. There's more variety now—artisan, world food, street food—and the number of markets is growing. But sustainability is the worry: new traders need advice and mentoring to survive.\nJoe Harrison: Catering or street‑food traders often need large up-front sums for vans, trailers and hygiene-compliant equipment—roughly £100,000 for that level of set-up.\nChair: Edward, how does that compare with starting a convenience store?\nEdward Woodall: Buying a going concern is typically priced at about ten times a shop's weekly turnover. Fitting an empty unit costs around £120–150k plus £40–60k for stock. Most new owners fund this from savings or family, not banks.\nMr Reynolds: How hard is it to secure a lease if you've been a manager but never owned a shop?\nEdward Woodall: Very hard. Landlords demand personal guarantees, often a year’s rent in advance, and leases are complex—new owners usually need legal help. That upfront risk deters first‑time operators.\nAlison Griffiths: Are returns reasonable? When do investors see payback?\nEdward Woodall: It usually takes two to three years to start recovering the initial investment and sometimes needs further investment along the way.\n\n\n## Markets, apprenticeships and getting people into jobs\n> Markets are inclusive and good for neurodiverse traders, but apprenticeships and work experience often fail to connect learners with small employers, hurting recruitment and growth.\n\nSarah Edwards: Lee, construction looks like an obvious opportunity for young people, but sentiment is bleak. Why aren't more joining?\nLee Wilcox: Many join by accident; construction isn’t championed in schools. Colleges are oversubscribed for construction courses, but many students then fail to transition into jobs—dropout-to-work rates are very high.\nLee Wilcox: The practical side is missing: SMEs who would employ apprentices face administration, cost and risk and often don’t have the time or information to take someone on.\nJoe Harrison: Markets are inclusive—about half the finalists in our young trader competition were neurodiverse. But growth requires practical experience; young people working part-time in markets learn valuable skills, and we should make that easier before 16.\nChris Bloore: Are apprenticeships working for small trades businesses?\nLee Wilcox: Not well enough. It's not the apprenticeship framework so much as the lack of connection between colleges and SMEs and the disincentives for small employers to take on apprentices in real workplaces.\n\n\n## Policy asks for tomorrow’s Budget\n> The witnesses want quicker payments to small suppliers, higher tax and rates thresholds, more mentoring delivered by trade bodies, and practical measures to help small employers hire apprentices.\n\nChris Bloore: What would you like to see announced at the Budget to encourage entrepreneurship?\nLee Wilcox: We welcomed guaranteed self‑employed paternity pay today, but we also want the VAT registration threshold doubled—so small trades don't suddenly face VAT paperwork and pricing disadvantages.\nJoe Harrison: Channel some Government funding through trade associations for mentoring and practical support. Associations know the sector and could help more small traders survive the critical first two to three years.\nEdward Woodall: Raise the small business rate relief threshold from a £12k rateable value to £18k so new small shop owners don't immediately pay business rates. Our sector’s bills have risen a lot because thresholds haven’t kept up.\nLee Wilcox: And please—SMEs must get paid faster. Quicker payments would relieve the biggest immediate pressure on cashflow for trades.\nChair: Those are clear, practical asks. Thank you all—the evidence will really help our inquiry.\n",
